<PageLayout title="Corrections" description="Sublime Pantry's public corrections log. When we get something wrong, the fix is recorded here." path="/corrections" breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Corrections", href: "/corrections" }]}>
  <span class="kicker">Policy</span>
  <h1>Corrections</h1>
  <p>When we publish an error of fact, we correct the article, add a correction note to it, and record the correction here with the date. Each entry names the affected page, what the earlier version stated and what replaced it. Style fixes and minor copy edits are not logged; factual corrections always are.</p>
  <p class="meta">The entries dated September 8, 2026 were found in a single internal review of the whole site, not reported by readers. They are logged on the date the corrections were published, not the date the errors were made.</p>
  <h2>Log</h2>
  <ol class="corrections-log">
    <li>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-09-06">September 6, 2026</time> — <a href="/compare/home-freeze-dryers">Home freeze dryers compared</a></p>
      <p><strong>Pump type stated incorrectly.</strong> Criterion 4 described the Harvest
      Right Premier as an oil-free pump and said it removed routine oil changes
      entirely. The Premier is an oil pump; oil-free is a priced upgrade on Harvest
      Right's own bundle listing. The Premier lengthens the oil-change interval — a
      change and filtering every 20-30 batches, against every 4-5 on the standard
      pump — rather than eliminating it.</p>
      <p>The error was a maintenance-cost misstatement material to a purchase
      decision, and it contradicted our own buying guide, which had the pump type
      right. Criterion 4 has been rewritten and the article carries a correction
      note. Found during an internal messaging audit, not reported by a reader.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-09-08">September 8, 2026</time> — <a href="/compare/fruit-vs-vegetables-vs-meat">Freeze-drying fruit, vegetables and meat</a></p>
      <p><strong>A household water-activity method presented as a pass/fail check.</strong> An
      earlier version gave a step-by-step procedure for reading water activity with a
      $10–25 digital hygrometer calibrated against saturated salt slurries, and
      described the result as a pass against 0.60. No extension service publishes that
      protocol as a validated home method, and a numbered procedure ending in a
      threshold reads as a test you can pass — which is what a consumer hygrometer
      cannot give you, least of all at the low end of the scale.</p>
      <p>The procedure has been removed and the page now points to the documented
      lower-cost method University of Minnesota Extension actually publishes. The same
      article's universal ≤0.25 aw target, which appeared in a diagram's description,
      its visible caption and an SVG label, has been removed from all three.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-09-08">September 8, 2026</time> — <a href="/troubleshooting/rehydration-problems">Rehydration problems</a> and <a href="/troubleshooting/storage-failure">storage failures</a></p>
      <p><strong>Recovery advice given before the handling history.</strong> Both pages
      recommended re-drying stored soft food before establishing how that food had been
      handled and for how long. A quality symptom cannot establish that food is safe to
      recover, and the food-group article compounded it by presenting fat oxidation as
      something that reliably precedes microbial risk.</p>
      <p>The handling-history decision now comes before any recovery advice, and the
      categorical rescue instruction and the relative safety timeline are gone.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-09-08">September 8, 2026</time> — <a href="/compare/home-freeze-dryers">Home freeze dryers compared</a></p>
      <p><strong>A warranty advantage that does not exist, and a wattage nobody
      publishes.</strong> The comparison listed Harvest Right's warranty as "3-year
      limited" and called it the clearest separation in the table. The sourced terms are
      one year of full coverage with the sealed system only in years two and three — the
      same shape of coverage the other two publish, not a separation. The same table
      gave Stay Fresh a "~900 W" average draw; Stay Fresh publishes no wattage for any
      home unit.</p>
      <p>Both figures have been withdrawn, the warranty row now reads by year and by
      component, and the machine specifications for all three brands are kept in one
      record that both machine pages are checked against.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-09-08">September 8, 2026</time> — <a href="/guides/which-freeze-dryer">Which home freeze dryer should you buy?</a> and <a href="/guides/cottage-economics">cottage economics</a></p>
      <p><strong>Two arithmetic errors visible without leaving the page.</strong> The
      buying guide called a $2,699 machine the cheapest medium option beside a $2,395
      one. The cottage-economics example stated that 360 bags at $8–12 gives $1,400–2,100
      a week; the same multiplication gives $2,880–4,320.</p>
      <p>The "cheapest" claim and the weekly revenue projection have both been removed.
      The transparent per-bag cost model they sat beside is unchanged, with its
      assumptions and its demand assumption now labelled separately.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-09-08">September 8, 2026</time> — <a href="/guides/which-freeze-dryer">Which home freeze dryer should you buy?</a> and <a href="/compare/fruit-vs-vegetables-vs-meat">the food comparison</a></p>
      <p><strong>Product lines described as available, and described wrongly.</strong>
      Both pages recommended "Pantry" kraft bags, and the food comparison described a
      Trail line as 7 mil silver mylar. Neither line is available to order, and the
      planned specification differs from what was published.</p>
      <p>Those selling paragraphs have been removed. The only product we sell is named
      where it is genuinely relevant, and it carries its ownership disclosure beside the
      mention.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-09-08">September 8, 2026</time> — <a href="/compare/fruit-vs-vegetables-vs-meat">Freeze-drying fruit, vegetables and meat</a></p>
      <p><strong>A USDA moisture figure we could not verify.</strong> An earlier version
      gave 2.0% as a USDA purchasing-specification moisture limit for freeze-dried food.
      The exact specification document is linked, but it is a scanned image with no
      readable text, so the figure could not be confirmed against its own source.</p>
      <p>The figure has been withdrawn rather than restated, and the question is recorded
      in the article's own account of what nobody has published.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>Spotted an error? <a href="/contact">Report it</a>. Send the page, the statement and a source if you have one; a report with a source is the fastest thing to act on.</p>
</PageLayout>
